  "details": "This probably isn't critical given we are in beta, but some people are going to naturally struggle with the new requirements and configuration Avatar 3.0 requires for animations to be setup (Animation logic, using the Unity Animation node system, how everything has to also be setup with a menu and parameters).\nCould we please get integrated into the avatar descriptor two default menu wheels, one for face emotions and one for emotes (dancing etc) That only require replacing the default animations with an equivalent from avatar 2.0. (minus the hand poses as those are no longer required now)\n(The wheels could have the names and icons as options, but should probably have the states for them all set to toggle and left out as an option as this is most similar to the way 2.0 works, less options overall will make this default simpler to use)\n(I would suggest each wheel is set to 8 animation slots as this is the equivalent we had in avatar 2.0, 8 hand gestures(emotions) and 8 emotes(dances) I don't know how easy this would be to implement but making the amount of slots a variable would make this default much more flexible)\nThis is simply to provide the same functionality that avatar 2.0 does, at the same accessible amount of unity knowledge, (the only knowledge that is required for 2.0 is how to make an animation and where to replace the defaults in the override). \nThis would also help people who simply want to implement a basic emotion, or replace a dance, without having to learn how to use, or go through the unity animation logic/node system.\nAnd make avatar 3.0 more accessible for people who struggle with these concepts and systems.\nIt would be naturally limited, but it would make it a lot easier for people who just want to upload an avatar with a couple of different common emotions (Happy, Sad etc) Without having to make a new action controller, expressions menu, stage parameter and then go through linking them all together.\nThere is a lot of new stuff involved to get Avatar 3.0 avatars to work as intended now. So naturally we need to make the system somewhat accessible, even if it is just a limited quick and dirty default option as suggested. Or people will be put off by it.",
